WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.933
Vector databases, the concept.

2
00:00:03.233 --> 00:00:07.767
How do you find the nearest vectors among millions, without checking every single one?

3
00:00:08.967 --> 00:00:12.000
Exact search compares the query against every vector.

4
00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:13.856
That's fine at ten thousand.

5
00:00:13.856 --> 00:00:15.800
It's hopeless at a hundred million.

6
00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:20.073
So vector databases use approximate nearest neighbor indexes.

7
00:00:20.073 --> 00:00:23.133
They give up a sliver of recall for huge speed.

8
00:00:24.333 --> 00:00:27.167
Here's the trade on one hundred thousand vectors.

9
00:00:27.467 --> 00:00:30.127
An exact scan takes about ten milliseconds.

10
00:00:30.127 --> 00:00:34.767
The graph index takes about a tenth of one, roughly a hundred times faster.

11
00:00:35.967 --> 00:00:38.800
The first index family is HNSW.

12
00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:43.967
Search enters a sparse top layer, and greedily hops toward the query with long jumps.

13
00:00:44.267 --> 00:00:47.033
Then it descends to a denser layer for shorter hops.

14
00:00:47.333 --> 00:00:50.028
Finally the bottom layer holds every vector.

15
00:00:50.028 --> 00:00:52.600
The search visits only a handful of nodes.

16
00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:55.633
The other family is IVF.

17
00:00:55.933 --> 00:00:57.895
HNSW is a layered graph.

18
00:00:57.895 --> 00:01:01.900
IVF partitions space into cells around centroids.

19
00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:04.073
HNSW hops and descends.

20
00:01:04.073 --> 00:01:07.167
IVF probes only the few nearest cells.

21
00:01:07.467 --> 00:01:10.200
Either way, a little recall buys a lot of speed.

22
00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:13.133
Here's the comparison in FAISS.

23
00:01:13.433 --> 00:01:15.733
One index scans everything exactly.

24
00:01:16.033 --> 00:01:18.600
The other builds an HNSW graph.

25
00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:22.400
We search both, and measure how many of the ten results match.

26
00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:27.067
The other half of a vector database is metadata filtering.

27
00:01:27.367 --> 00:01:31.396
Mission Control RAG first retrieved the top ten, then filtered.

28
00:01:31.396 --> 00:01:35.233
For Artemis queries, all ten were often from other missions.

29
00:01:35.533 --> 00:01:37.467
Filtering afterwards left nothing.

30
00:01:37.767 --> 00:01:41.384
Moving the filter into the query, during the walk, fixed it.

31
00:01:41.384 --> 00:01:44.700
Post-filtering top-k is a classic first production bug.

32
00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:46.671
Let's recap.

33
00:01:46.671 --> 00:01:50.400
Exact search doesn't scale, so we use approximate indexes.

34
00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:52.962
HNSW hops through layers.

35
00:01:52.962 --> 00:01:54.500
IVF probes cells.

36
00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:57.000
Both trade a sliver of recall for speed.

37
00:01:57.300 --> 00:01:59.996
And filter during the search, never after.

38
00:01:59.996 --> 00:02:01.600
Next up: semantic search.
